    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_epidermis",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Epidermis",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Epidermis",
        "margin_note": "Name the three regions in one line, then take them one at a time.",
        "lines": [
          "A transverse section of a typical young",
          "dicotyledonous stem shows three regions:",
          "I. Epidermis    II. Cortex    III. Stele",
          {
            "text": "I. Epidermis",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "It is the outermost single layer of cells and",
          "is the protective layer of the stem.",
          "It is covered on the outside by a thin layer",
          "of cuticle.",
          "It may bear trichomes, that is epidermal",
          "hairs, and a few stomata."
        ],
        "why": "The section is read from the outside inward, so naming the three regions first fixes the order of the whole answer and stops a layer from being missed.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Starting with the vascular bundles. The examiner follows the section from outside inward.",
          "Leaving out the cuticle and the trichomes. Both belong to this mark."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Outside to inside: Epidermis, Cortex, Stele."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_cortex",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Cortex — three zones",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Three cortical zones",
        "margin_note": "Two marks here. Use the letters a, b, c so the three zones are countable.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "II. Cortex",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The cells lying in several layers between the",
          "epidermis and the pericycle make the cortex.",
          "It has three sub-zones.",
          "a) Hypodermis: a few layers of collenchyma",
          "cells lying just below the epidermis. It gives",
          "mechanical strength to the young stem.",
          "b) General cortex: it lies below the",
          "hypodermis and is made of rounded, thin walled",
          "parenchyma cells with clear intercellular",
          "spaces.",
          "c) Endodermis: the innermost single layer of",
          "the cortex. Its cells are rich in starch",
          "grains, so this layer is also called the",
          "starch sheath."
        ],
        "why": "Each zone is named for the job it does at that depth: collenchyma outside where bending strength is needed, thin walled parenchyma in the middle for storage, and a starch rich layer marking the inner boundary.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing sclerenchyma for the hypodermis of a dicot stem. In a dicot stem it is collenchyma.",
          "Missing the third zone. The endodermis is part of the cortex, not of the stele.",
          "Forgetting the other name for the endodermis. Starch sheath is a marked word."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Hypodermis, general cortex, endodermis: strong, then soft, then starchy."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_pericycle",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Pericycle",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Pericycle",
        "margin_note": "Open the stele here. Name its four contents before describing the pericycle.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "III. Stele",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "All the tissue lying on the inner side of the",
          "endodermis is the stele. It consists of the",
          "pericycle, the vascular bundles, the medulla",
          "and the medullary rays.",
          "a) Pericycle: it lies on the inner side of the",
          "endodermis.",
          "Above each patch of phloem it is present as a",
          "semilunar patch of sclerenchyma, which is",
          "called the bundle cap."
        ],
        "why": "The pericycle is not a continuous ring of one tissue here; it is thickened only over the phloem, and that thickened patch is what a student must be able to name in the diagram.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Drawing the pericycle as a complete sclerenchyma ring. In a dicot stem it is in semilunar patches.",
          "Placing the bundle cap below the xylem. It lies above the phloem, on the outer side."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "A cap sits on top, so the bundle cap sits on the outer side, over the phloem."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_bundles",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Vascular bundles",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Bundles described",
        "margin_note": "Write the four technical words in full. They are what the mark is for.",
        "lines": [
          "b) Vascular bundles: a large number of bundles",
          "are arranged in a ring. This ring arrangement",
          "is the character of a dicot stem.",
          "Each bundle is conjoint, that is xylem and",
          "phloem lie in the same bundle.",
          "Each bundle is collateral, that is phloem lies",
          "on the outer side and xylem on the inner side.",
          "Each bundle is open, that is a strip of",
          "cambium lies between the xylem and the phloem,",
          "so secondary growth is possible.",
          "The protoxylem lies towards the centre and the",
          "metaxylem towards the outside, so the xylem is",
          "endarch."
        ],
        "why": "Conjoint, collateral, open and endarch are four separate claims about one bundle, and each is a phrase the examiner looks for. Open is the one that matters most, because it is what allows a dicot stem to thicken.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing closed for a dicot stem. A dicot stem bundle is open.",
          "Writing exarch. Exarch belongs to the root; a stem is endarch.",
          "Putting the xylem outside and the phloem inside."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Ring, conjoint, collateral, open, endarch: five words that describe a dicot stem bundle."
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_pith_rays",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Medulla and medullary rays",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Pith and rays",
        "margin_note": "Close with the one-line summary of the bundle. That is the sentence the examiner looks for.",
        "lines": [
          "c) Medulla or pith: it occupies the central",
          "portion of the stem. It is made of a large",
          "number of rounded parenchyma cells with large",
          "intercellular spaces.",
          "d) Medullary rays: in between two vascular",
          "bundles there are a few layers of radially",
          "placed parenchyma cells. These constitute the",
          "medullary rays.",
          "They carry water and food from the centre",
          "towards the outside.",
          {
            "text": "Bundles in a ring, conjoint, collateral, open, endarch",
            "style": "boxed"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Both are parenchyma; what separates them is position. The pith is the block left in the centre and the rays are the strips left between the bundles, so once the ring of bundles is drawn, both follow from the drawing.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Calling the medullary rays vascular tissue. They are parenchyma.",
          "Saying a dicot stem has no pith. The pith is large and central."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Same tissue, two positions: the middle is pith, the strips between bundles are rays."
      },
